**What I checked explicitly:**
- The equivalence in step 1.1: universality of a net means by definition that it is eventually in \(S\) or eventually in \(X\setminus S\) for every \(S\subseteq X\). By [A1] (the tail‑filter property) this is exactly that the tail filter contains \(S\) or \(X\setminus S\). By [A2] (ultrafilter characterisation) a filter is an ultrafilter if and only if it has that property; hence the net is universal iff its tail filter is an ultrafilter. The chaining of equivalences is correct.
- Step 1.2: for an ultrafilter \(\mathcal U\) and any \(S\subseteq X\), [A2] gives \(S\in\mathcal U\) or \(X\setminus S\in\mathcal U\). In the first case \(S\) is non‑empty (filters contain no empty set), so an index \((S,a)\) exists in the derived net. The order definition (summarised in [A3]) makes every later index have first coordinate contained in \(S\); because the net’s value is the second coordinate and that coordinate belongs to the first coordinate by construction, all later values lie in \(S\). The second case is symmetric. Thus the derived net is eventually in \(S\) or eventually in \(X\setminus S\) for every \(S\), i.e. it is universal. No hidden choice principle is required (the existence of an \(a\in S\) follows from \(S\neq\varnothing\) without AC).
